23:19:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you—when you see the same pattern repeatedly, it can feel like an undeniable trend. I’ve definitely had moments where I thought, “Why do people keep doing this?” and started to generalize. But let’s unpack that a bit. What you’re describing might be a mix of a few things: - **Confirmation bias:** Once you start expecting guys to do something “dumb,” your brain becomes very good at noticing every instance that fits the story and ignoring the ones that don’t. You might also be more likely to label a man’s mistake as “dumb” while interpreting a similar mistake by someone of a different gender as just a simple error or bad luck. - **The workplace context:** If your team is mostly male, then statistically most of the “dumb” moments you witness will come from men, simply because they’re the majority around you. That doesn’t mean the behavior is inherent to being male. - **What counts as “dumb”:** Often, what we call “dumb” is actually a very human thing—distraction, overconfidence, not asking for clarification, or rushing. Those aren’t exclusive to any gender; they’re just easier to see in a group that’s well-represented in our immediate environment. I’m not dismissing your experience—it’s real and frustrating. But using that feeling to design training for an entire team based on a stereotype (even if it feels earned) can backfire. It can come across as condescending, lower engagement, and actually make people less receptive to the material. Plus, you risk missing the actual root causes of the mistakes you’re trying to prevent. A more useful approach might be to ask: *What specific recurring “dumb” actions am I seeing?* Are they skipping steps? Ignoring documentation? Making assumptions? Then train against *those behaviors* with clear structure, checklists, and examples—things that help *anyone* who’s prone to those human slip-ups, regardless of gender. That way, you fix the real problem without relying on an unfair generalization.
